WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.502 --> 00:00:08.474
Hey, everybody. Welcome
to "Corrections: Remote."

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:11.844
Because I am vaccinated...

00:00:11.844 --> 00:00:15.815
but not Jacksinated --
vaccinated against Jackals.

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:22.021
We are gonna do Corrections
here in a stranger's apartment.

00:00:22.021 --> 00:00:23.823
Um...

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:27.460
This is basically
a month's worth of Corrections

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.796
because the last one we did
was right before Christmas.

00:00:30.796 --> 00:00:36.502
And pretty -- pretty big boner
by me early on,

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:42.074
saying that the "Mona Lisa"
was painted by Michelangelo,

00:00:42.074 --> 00:00:46.545
when a lot of you seem to think
it was da Vinci,

00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:49.816
but they're pretty --

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:53.419
they're pretty hard
to tell apart, those two.

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:57.557
I mean, especially when they're
not wearing their bandanas.

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:02.295
You know who
my hero in a half shell is?

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.130
Botticelli.

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:08.902
Um, more art-history flubs.

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:14.840
We talked
about Edvard Munch's "Scream."

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:19.112
I said the guy in the painting
wasn't screaming;

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:21.313
he was reacting to a scream.

00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:25.919
A very wise, well-informed
Jackal sent me something

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:28.487
I'm gonna read you right now

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:33.626
clarifying exactly what was
happening in that painting.

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:37.496
They wrote, "In fact, Munch
wrote a passage in his journal

00:01:37.496 --> 00:01:39.565
prior to the painting.

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:41.667
'I was walking along the road
with two friends.

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:44.970
The sun went down.
I felt a gust of melancholy.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.673
Suddenly, the sky
turned a bloody red.

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:49.842
I stopped, leaned
against the railing,

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:52.077
tired to death
as the flaming skies

00:01:52.077 --> 00:01:53.746
hung like blood and sword

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:57.183
over the blue-black fjord
and the city.

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:58.484
My friends went on.

00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:00.687
I stood there
trembling with anxiety,

00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:02.121
and I felt a vast,

00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:06.358
infinite scream
through nature.'"

00:02:06.358 --> 00:02:08.361
Overdramatic Munch?

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:11.697
[ Laughter ]

00:02:11.697 --> 00:02:15.834
I mentioned Thomas Jefferson...

00:02:15.834 --> 00:02:18.204
as an inventor.

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:20.873
And people took issue with that.

00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:23.275
They said, "I think
you meant Thomas Edison

00:02:23.275 --> 00:02:25.879
or maybe even
Benjamin Franklin."

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:27.747
But I'm here to tell you --

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:33.085
Thomas Jefferson was
a very accomplished inventor.

00:02:33.085 --> 00:02:34.487
Polygraph.

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:36.322
Not like a lie-detector
polygraph,

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:39.158
but a poly-- where you can
write a letter in duplicate

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:41.293
'cause the pens were connected.

00:02:41.293 --> 00:02:46.398
This is all at Monticello,
the home of limoncello.

00:02:46.398 --> 00:02:47.733
And so the polygraph.

00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:50.837
The swivel chair
was Thomas Jefferson.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:53.305
And the Jefferson disc,

00:02:53.305 --> 00:02:57.076
which is a CD
that plays "White Rabbit."

00:02:57.076 --> 00:03:00.513
[ Laughter ]

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:03.516
There was some confusion
based on something

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:06.552
that happened
in the last Corrections.

00:03:06.552 --> 00:03:09.221
I was talking
about the difficulty

00:03:09.221 --> 00:03:13.526
of doing
a Hugh Laurie impression

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:16.095
while he was playing House,

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:17.963
because then I have to do
an impression

00:03:17.963 --> 00:03:19.398
of a British person
who's then doing an impression

00:03:19.398 --> 00:03:20.733
of an American person.

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:24.603
And then I did a Hugh Grant.

00:03:24.603 --> 00:03:26.805
People thought I thought
Hugh Grant played Hugh Laurie.

00:03:26.805 --> 00:03:28.140
That's just -- That was --

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.775
The problem there
is that "Hugh"

00:03:29.775 --> 00:03:33.880
is too popular amongst
British actors as a first name.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.382
I know the difference between
Hugh Laurie and Hugh Grant.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:38.818
You've got Hugh Dancy,
Hugh Bonneville.

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:42.154
There's a lot of Hughs.
There's too many Hughs.

00:03:42.154 --> 00:03:45.825
I will say I think
Hugh is a perfect --

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:47.159
It's like a per--

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:51.931
It's the perfect British name
because it sounds like

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:53.933
what the Dowager Countess
would say

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:57.570
if she didn't know a person.

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:02.541
"Have you met Mr. Grant?"

00:04:02.541 --> 00:04:04.343
"Hugh?!"

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:06.278
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.278 --> 00:04:08.013
That was pretty good.

00:04:08.013 --> 00:04:14.454
Oh, I said the anime series
"Sailor Moon" had been canceled,

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:16.822
and I was assured
by fans of the series

00:04:16.822 --> 00:04:21.260
that it was not canceled;
it just completed the story.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:26.299
This was also true of
NBC's sitcom "Animal Practice."

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:28.401
[ Laughter ]

00:04:30.302 --> 00:04:34.440
I said the word "timbre."
I pronounced it "timber."

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.043
I did that thing
where I made the error

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:39.545
of pronouncing it
the way it was spelled.

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:44.183
A lot of you said my Ric Flair
impression was bad.

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:46.319
It was terrible,
and I want to apologize

00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:50.222
to the Flair family, Flair fans.

00:04:50.222 --> 00:04:54.093
What happened is, we --
impression --

00:04:54.093 --> 00:04:55.895
Impression camp
had to go to remote,

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:58.698
and it's just -- I just don't
get the same instruction

00:04:58.698 --> 00:04:59.999
via Zoom.

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:02.801
And so there's been, um --

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:05.672
There's been a fall-off.

00:05:05.672 --> 00:05:09.142
Okay.

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:13.912
I talked about how Ted Cruz

00:05:13.912 --> 00:05:17.716
looks like "a Chewbacca."

00:05:17.716 --> 00:05:21.820
Chewbacca is not
a species of creature.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.424
Chewbacca is a name.

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:28.828
So shame on me.

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:30.596
That was a Wookiee mistake.

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:36.135
[ Laughter ]

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:37.970
We also showed -- when we were
talking about Ted Cruz,

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:41.774
we showed a skateboard photo
for a skateboard fail.

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:43.475
Can we -- Can we throw
that up real quick, Alex?

00:05:43.475 --> 00:05:46.345
And, uh...

00:05:46.345 --> 00:05:50.683
someone had -- the criticism
they had of this picture

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:53.553
is that the railing
makes a shadow,

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:57.956
but the skateboarder
doesn't make a shadow.

00:05:57.956 --> 00:06:00.025
You know what that means?

00:06:00.025 --> 00:06:01.694
He's a Dracula.

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:06.365
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:10.336
Clip It,
better known as Clippy.

00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:17.977
Talked about the fact
that he was introduced in 1997.

00:06:17.977 --> 00:06:22.214
I was told by many of you
that he was introduced

00:06:22.214 --> 00:06:26.252
in Office '97,
but that came out in 1996,

00:06:26.252 --> 00:06:32.992
which seems like a correction
Microsoft should be making.

00:06:32.992 --> 00:06:35.561
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:37.930
Okay.
We were talking about

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:42.934
how the CDC issued
a travel advisory to Canada.

00:06:42.934 --> 00:06:45.637
We mentioned this
in a monologue,

00:06:45.637 --> 00:06:47.673
and a lot of you said,

00:06:47.673 --> 00:06:51.610
that's not a map of Canada,
that's a map of Georgia.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:53.212
So I thought, "What?

00:06:53.212 --> 00:06:56.749
Why would we put up
a map of Georgia

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:58.184
when we were telling
a joke about Canada?"

00:06:58.184 --> 00:06:59.518
And then I went back
and I looked at the joke,

00:06:59.518 --> 00:07:01.220
and I realize what happened,
which is the CDC

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:04.690
is located in Atlanta,
so the graphics departments

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:09.261
thought the best representation
for that joke would be

00:07:09.261 --> 00:07:14.066
to show the state
that the CDC is in,

00:07:14.066 --> 00:07:16.368
because what the graphics
department often is,

00:07:16.368 --> 00:07:21.240
is technically correct
and practically irritating.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:22.841
[ Laughter ]

00:07:22.841 --> 00:07:25.778
But that wasn't the worst thing
Graphics got me on.

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:28.281
We talked about how there is a

00:07:28.281 --> 00:07:31.884
tree named after
Leonardo DiCaprio

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:35.587
and that that tree
is dating a sapling.

00:07:35.587 --> 00:07:40.092
Then we showed a photo
of a seedling, which is, um...

00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:42.328
That's not the kind of joke
we're trying to make here, okay?

00:07:42.328 --> 00:07:43.429
[ Laughter ]

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:46.132
About somebody
hitting on a seedling.

00:07:46.132 --> 00:07:51.103
That crosses the line,
and it shouldn't happen.

00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:54.674
Apologize to Mr. DiCaprio
and all the tree enthusiasts

00:07:54.674 --> 00:07:58.578
out there
who had to stomach that joke.

00:07:58.578 --> 00:08:00.679
Now, in normal times, of course,

00:08:00.679 --> 00:08:01.980
what would happen is,
I would march down --

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:06.485
I would march down to Graphics,
and I would, um...

00:08:06.485 --> 00:08:09.088
have a word with them,
but we can't do that right now.

00:08:09.088 --> 00:08:11.189
I'm here.
No need to tell you that.

00:08:11.189 --> 00:08:14.192
And so instead,

00:08:14.192 --> 00:08:15.994
I set up a Zoom call
with them.

00:08:15.994 --> 00:08:18.864
Now, a lot of people made jokes
about the fact

00:08:18.864 --> 00:08:21.734
that you don't have to
wear pants during Zoom calls.

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.902
Sure. Right?
You don't.

00:08:23.902 --> 00:08:29.274
But the problem with Graphics
is, one, they don't wear pants.

00:08:29.274 --> 00:08:31.610
Which wouldn't matter,
except they do all their Zoom

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:33.446
calls standing up.

00:08:33.446 --> 00:08:36.348
[ Laughter ]

00:08:36.348 --> 00:08:38.551
And they also turn
their mics on

00:08:38.551 --> 00:08:40.018
before they turn
on their screens,

00:08:40.018 --> 00:08:41.320
and so they make sure --

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:45.024
You don't know what's coming,
but they make sure

00:08:45.024 --> 00:08:46.491
you're watching.

00:08:46.491 --> 00:08:49.361
You know, I start the Zoom,
obviously pretty angry.

00:08:49.361 --> 00:08:50.696
I'm like, "All right, guys,
let's get this started,"

00:08:50.696 --> 00:08:52.264
and they're like,
"Are you there?"

00:08:52.264 --> 00:08:54.633
I'm like, "Yeah, I'm there.
Let's get this going.

00:08:54.633 --> 00:08:56.101
Turn on your cameras."

00:08:56.101 --> 00:08:58.236
And then it's just like,
click, click, click,

00:08:58.236 --> 00:09:01.574
and you see...everything.

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:03.175
[ Laughter ]

00:09:03.175 --> 00:09:08.413
I mentioned that horse pudding,

00:09:08.413 --> 00:09:11.517
the flavor was Chocolateriat.
And then, well, you guys,

00:09:11.517 --> 00:09:15.988
you can see that the flavor
actually says "original."

00:09:15.988 --> 00:09:20.893
And, um, that's on me
because I did not give --

00:09:20.893 --> 00:09:23.562
I did not give our
props department enough time.

00:09:23.562 --> 00:09:27.933
I asked them to make me
this horse pudding.

00:09:27.933 --> 00:09:32.871
And by the time
I asked them to change the label

00:09:32.871 --> 00:09:34.873
from original to Chocolateriat,
it was too late.

00:09:34.873 --> 00:09:37.542
And this was --
because it was...

00:09:37.542 --> 00:09:42.481
Sometimes, again, when you
have meetings in person,

00:09:42.481 --> 00:09:44.850
I feel like you
you don't miss as much.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:47.819
But, again, this was all done
via e-mail and text

00:09:47.819 --> 00:09:49.621
'cause I'm not in the office,
and so when I ask them

00:09:49.621 --> 00:09:53.726
for horse pudding,
they didn't just, like, buy

00:09:53.726 --> 00:09:55.194
chocolate Jell-O pudding
and change the label.

00:09:55.194 --> 00:09:58.263
They...

00:09:58.263 --> 00:09:59.532
I don't even want to say this,

00:09:59.532 --> 00:10:00.933
because, like, if somebody
from PETA is watching,

00:10:00.933 --> 00:10:03.869
I find, like, what
they did is disturbing.

00:10:03.869 --> 00:10:05.503
[ Laughter ]

00:10:05.503 --> 00:10:08.140
I asked them for horse pudding,
and I got horse pudding.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:09.441
[ Laughter ]

00:10:09.441 --> 00:10:14.013
I don't want to talk any more
about what happened.

00:10:14.013 --> 00:10:15.648
[ Laughter ]

00:10:15.648 --> 00:10:17.682
Okay?

00:10:17.682 --> 00:10:21.253
It's been a hard week.

00:10:21.253 --> 00:10:23.622
For everyone.

00:10:23.622 --> 00:10:29.662
And with that said,
pretty excited

00:10:29.662 --> 00:10:34.800
to get back into the studio
on Monday, and...

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:38.303
keep watching,
keep looking for the flubs.

00:10:38.303 --> 00:10:42.574
And let's try to make this year,
2022, a big year

00:10:42.574 --> 00:10:45.010
with the fewest
corrections ever.

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:47.279
You guys are gonna see me
next week.

